# Amendment #34 — memory-system eager lifespan / D1 health-check conformance

2026-04-25. Closes the D1 conformance defect amendment #24's
FastAPI → FastMCP transport swap silently introduced: under the
new transport, the user lifespan that builds Graphiti is no
longer invoked at process start — FastMCP routes it to the
lower-level `MCPServer.run`, which is invoked per MCP session by
`StreamableHTTPSessionManager`. Until a client opens an MCP
session, the module-level `_graphiti` stays `None` and the
`GET /health` Starlette `custom_route` returns 503
`{"status":"initialising"}` indefinitely. That defeats hands-off-
lifecycle's phase-4b probe (60s polling without ever opening an
MCP session) and — independently — fails the spec-level D1 clause
"exposes a health check," because a route that never returns
healthy is not exposing a health check in any meaningful sense.

Re-extension of amendment #24's AC24.1 per ODD §4. AC24.1 directly
invoked `service.lifespan(service.mcp)` as an async context manager
(proves the lifespan body works when entered) but said nothing
about whether the framework actually entered it at process start.
AC34.1 promotes the outcome-shaped behaviour to a named criterion.
Amendment #24's seal stays untouched (no retroactive change to its
AC list); amendment #34 lands the missing AC.

Structural remedy (plan §3 D5 option 1 — pre-build before serve):

  - New module-level coroutine `_ensure_graphiti()` constructs
    Graphiti idempotently (`if _graphiti is not None: return`).
    Body equivalent to the lifespan's construct half (`load_env`,
    `make_graphiti`, `build_indices_and_constraints`).
  - `lifespan` body's construct half now delegates to
    `_ensure_graphiti()`. Per-session enters of the lifespan
    observe the already-populated `_graphiti` and skip rebuild.
    The yield/finally shape preserves; close-on-exit semantics
    intact.
  - `run()` calls `await _ensure_graphiti()` before
    `await mcp.run_streamable_http_async()`. Both run inside the
    same `asyncio.run` so the event-loop is shared. `_graphiti` is
    populated before uvicorn begins accepting requests; `/health`
    returns 200 from the first GET onwards.

The Starlette router's modern lifespan-context shape exposes no
post-hoc `add_event_handler` / `on_startup`, so option (2) was
rejected as more surface than option (1). Empirically verified
via `app.router.on_startup` raising `AttributeError` on the
FastMCP-returned Starlette app.

Method-level choices (builder's call per ODD §1.1):

  - Test shape (D4): subprocess-spawn + plain HTTP GET, mirroring
    AC29.4's bind-only subprocess pattern. Subprocess swaps
    `service.make_graphiti` and `service.load_env` for fakes
    (no Ollama / Kuzu / Claude). Parent test polls
    `127.0.0.1:<port>/health` until 200 with `workspace_root`
    field, within a 5-second budget. No MCP session opened.
  - Eager-init shape (D5): option (1) pre-build before serve.
    Idempotency guard via `if _graphiti is not None: return`.

Fail-closed behaviours preserved:

  - The per-session lifespan path keeps working — `lifespan`
    observes `_graphiti` already populated and skips rebuild.
  - `/health` route's 503 response when `_graphiti is None`
    remains the fall-back for any path that bypasses
    `_ensure_graphiti()`. Under the post-fix flow, that path is
    no longer reachable in production.
  - `_require_graphiti()` still raises if `_graphiti is None`;
    tool implementations preserve their pre-fix surface.
  - If `make_graphiti` fails at startup (Ollama unreachable, Kuzu
    locked, etc.), the failure surfaces at uvicorn startup — the
    process exits, launchd's `KeepAlive` restarts it, and the
    cycle repeats inside `KeepAlive`'s back-off. Pre-fix the
    failure was deferred to the first MCP call; post-fix the
    failure is eager. Per plan §R1, this is acceptable — the
    user-facing diagnostic at phase-4b is `service-health-timeout`
    either way.

AC surface (AC34.1–AC34.3):

  - AC34.1: `python -m src.service` subprocess started under a
    FakeGraphiti seam returns 200 OK with `workspace_root` field
    from `GET /health` within 5s of subprocess spawn, without an
    MCP session being opened. Test:
    `memory-system/tests/test_AC34_eager_health_after_startup.py
    ::test_AC34_1_health_returns_200_after_subprocess_serve_loop_entry`.
  - AC34.2: AC24.1–AC24.7, AC29.4, AC29.5 stay green after the
    fix. Pointer-to-evidence test
    (`test_AC34_2_no_regression_on_AC24_and_AC29`) exercises the
    AC24.1 lifespan-construct test + AC29.5 health-workspace-root
    test inline; the suite-level run of the underlying tests is
    the primary gate.
  - AC34.3: seal-diff invariant — `git diff --name-only
    BASELINE..HEAD` for memory-system shows only paths under
    `memory-system/` or universal-paths. Pointer-to-evidence test
    (`test_AC34_3_seal_diff_only_memory_system_changed`) duplicates
    the existing `test_B20_*` assertion under an AC34-named
    function so the outcome is callable by name.

Preserved failure-class closures:

  - Amendment #24 MCP transport + four-tool surface unchanged.
  - Amendment #25 BGE reranker default unchanged.
  - Amendment #29 per-workspace-port + identity-aware probe
    unchanged; AC34.1's eager init makes AC29.5's probe actually
    CI-exercisable (it currently only works for tests that bypass
    the launchd path and directly enter the lifespan).
  - Amendment #30 env-scrubber USER admission unchanged.

Out of scope (per plan §8):

  - No changes to workspace-identity machinery (amendments #6,
    #28, #29).
  - No changes to graphiti-core, kuzu-driver, BGE reranker, LLM
    client, embedder.
  - No changes to MCP transport shape, tool surface, or
    `streamable_http_path` setting.
  - No changes to hands-off-lifecycle's phase-4b probe.
  - No changes to workspace-bootstrap's launchd plist template.
  - No new launchd-level health check.
  - No spec v1.x amendment. D1 unchanged; this is a conformance fix.

